      <h2>6. Technical Challenges of Group Video Chat</h2>

      <p>Group communication is technically more demanding than a simple one-to-one call because the system has to coordinate multiple participants and media streams.</p>

      <h3>6.1 Audio Management</h3>

      <p>When several microphones are active, background noise and overlapping speech can make conversations difficult to understand. Echo cancellation, noise suppression and microphone controls can improve the experience.</p>

      <h3>6.2 Video Layout</h3>

      <p>A room needs to display several participants while remaining usable on different screen sizes. A good interface should adapt when participants join, leave or change their camera state.</p>

      <h3>6.3 Network Conditions</h3>

      <p>Participants may have very different internet speeds. Adaptive media techniques can help maintain a usable connection when bandwidth changes.</p>

      <h3>6.4 Server Capacity</h3>

      <p>As room activity increases, the infrastructure may need additional resources to handle signaling, media routing, room state and other services.</p>

      <h3>6.5 Mobile Constraints</h3>

      <p>Phones have smaller screens, changing network conditions and limited battery compared with desktop computers. Mobile-friendly room interfaces therefore need to prioritize controls and media layout carefully.</p>

