      <h2>7. Educational and Professional Uses of Talk Rooms</h2>

      <p>Talk rooms can support several types of collaborative activity when participants have a clear purpose and appropriate moderation.</p>

      <h3>7.1 Language Exchange</h3>

      <p>Language learners can use live rooms to practice listening and speaking with other participants. Group settings can expose learners to different accents, speaking styles and vocabulary.</p>

      <h3>7.2 Study Discussions</h3>

      <p>Students can use discussion rooms to exchange explanations, ask questions and discuss topics outside a traditional classroom.</p>

      <h3>7.3 Brainstorming</h3>

      <p>Small teams can use live rooms to discuss ideas and receive immediate feedback from other participants.</p>

      <h3>7.4 Knowledge Sharing</h3>

      <p>Interest-based rooms can bring together people with different levels of experience. Participants can share examples, ask questions and compare approaches.</p>

      <h3>7.5 Community Discussions</h3>

      <p>Rooms can provide a more interactive format for communities that would otherwise communicate through text-only posts.</p>

